USER SECTION (My Tasks — resource/team-member login) — backend needs
========================================================================

Scope: /my-tasks and /my-tasks/:projectId pages, used by non-PM logins
(tester, tech lead, architect, etc.) to see their own assigned work.

Frontend is now production-ready on its own side — every screen shows
real data where the backend provides it, and an honest "Not available" /
"Not tracked yet" label anywhere it doesn't (no invented names, dates, or
percentages). The 4 gaps below are the only things blocking full data.


1. get_userProjectList needs more fields
------------------------------------------
Current (confirmed live): POST { resource: <user id> } returns
  { id, sub_project_id, project_id, resource, created_at, planned_start,
    planned_end, duration, predecessor, project_name, sub_project_name, type }

Missing fields the UI needs and currently shows as "Not available"/"Not set":
  - pm_id            (which PM owns this project — needed for #3 below)
  - priority          (High/Medium/Low)
  - category / domain (what kind of work this project is)
  - manager_name      (project's manager, shown in Project Overview)
  - client_name       (shown in Project Overview)
  - status / is_completed (so "Completed" work can actually be counted —
    right now there is no way to know if a task is done)
  - updated_at        (last-modified time, shown in List tab's "Updated" column)
  - task_key          (short id like "PJ-16", shown in List tab's "Key" column)
  - reporter_name     (who raised/assigned the task, List tab's "Reporter" column)
  - comment_count     (or a real per-task comments endpoint, for List tab's
    "Comment" column — no per-task commenting exists at all today)

Requested response shape (adds fields, doesn't remove any existing ones):
  {
    "success": true,
    "data": [
      {
        "id": 55, "project_id": 12, "sub_project_id": null,
        "resource": 4, "created_at": "2026-08-01",
        "planned_start": "2026-08-10", "planned_end": "2026-08-20",
        "duration": 10, "predecessor": "",
        "project_name": "Website Revamp", "sub_project_name": null,
        "type": "project",
        "pm_id": 19,
        "priority": "High",
        "category": "Website",
        "manager_name": "Ashwini Singh",
        "client_name": "Acme Corp",
        "status": "in_progress"
      }
    ]
  }


2. Board tab can't load for a resource/team-member
-----------------------------------------------------
getBoard (POST /projectManager/getBoard) requires { pm_id, project_id }.
A resource user only knows their own id and the project_id — not the
project's pm_id. Once get_userProjectList returns pm_id (see #1), this is
already solved on our side — no separate fix needed here IF #1 ships.

If pm_id can't be added to get_userProjectList for some reason, the
alternative is: let getBoard accept { project_id } alone (resolve pm_id
internally on the backend, since a project has exactly one PM anyway).


3. Backlog is PM-only today, not project-scoped
---------------------------------------------------
get_pmBacklog (POST /projectManager/get_pmBacklog) takes { pm_id } and
returns that PM's entire backlog across all their projects. It assumes
the logged-in user's own id IS a pm_id — which is only true for a PM
login. For a resource/team-member login this is wrong (and risky: if a
resource's numeric id happens to match a real PM's id, it would show
that PM's backlog by mistake).

Needed: a project-scoped backlog fetch, e.g.
  POST /projectManager/get_projectBacklog  { project_id: 12 }
  -> { success, data: [ ...backlog items for that project only... ] }

Until this exists, the Backlog tab is intentionally disabled for
resource/team-member logins on the frontend (shows "not available for
your account yet" instead of risking wrong data).


4. Discussions aren't filtered by project
----------------------------------------------
get_userDiscussion (POST /users/get_userDiscussion, form-data:
resource_id, type) returns ALL of a resource's discussions mixed
together — there's no project_id in the request or a reliable project_id
field in each response row to filter by client-side (live test only
returned project_name/project_scope/start_date/end_date/id — id's
meaning is ambiguous, could be the project's own id or a discussion
row's id).

Needed: accept project_id as a filter param, e.g.
  POST /users/get_userDiscussion  (form-data) resource_id, type, project_id
  -> only discussions belonging to that project

Until this ships, the Discussions tab on a project's detail page shows
discussions from every project the resource is on, not just this one.


NICE TO HAVE (not blocking, lower priority)
---------------------------------------------
- A real "health index" / project-status metric, if that feature is
  wanted — right now the Project Status tab just shows Planned
  Start/Deadline (real) and marks Health Index as "Not available"
  because no such field exists anywhere.
- A task-type field beyond project/subproject (e.g. Bug/Epic/Story) if
  "Types of work" breakdown should show more than Task vs Subtask.
